        <table-wrap id="article-31464.table1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 2. Examples of Drugs Exhibiting Mixed-Order (Nonlinear)</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 26.6667px;">
                <td style="width: 69px; height: 26.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Drug</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 177.698px; height: 26.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Km / Metabolic Characteristics</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 119.302px; height: 26.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Kinetic Behavior</bold>
</td>
                <td style="width: 158px; height: 26.6667px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<bold>Clinical Implications</bold>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 40px;">
                <td style="width: 69px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Phenytoin</td>
                <td style="width: 177.698px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Km = Concentration where metabolism is half-maximal; close to therapeutic levels, so elimination saturates easily</td>
                <td style="width: 119.302px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">First-order at low doses; zero-order near/above therapeutic levels</td>
                <td style="width: 158px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Small dose changes &#x02192; large plasma level changes; narrow therapeutic window; toxicity risk</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 40px;">
                <td style="width: 69px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ethanol</td>
                <td style="width: 177.698px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Km is very high, so enzyme saturation occurs at low plasma concentrations, resulting in a near-constant elimination rate</td>
                <td style="width: 119.302px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Almost purely zero-order due to enzyme saturation at typical intake</td>
                <td style="width: 158px; height: 40px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Constant elimination rate; risk of accumulation with overdose</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="width: 69px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Theophylline</td>
                <td style="width: 177.698px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Km is near therapeutic levels,&#x000a0;so elimination saturates at high concentrations</td>
                <td style="width: 119.302px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">First-order at low doses; zero-order at toxic levels</td>
                <td style="width: 158px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Toxicity risk rises steeply above the therapeutic range</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="width: 69px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Salicylates</td>
                <td style="width: 177.698px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Km is much higher than analgesic doses, but saturates at anti-inflammatory or toxic doses</td>
                <td style="width: 119.302px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">First-order at low doses; zero-order at high/overdose levels</td>
                <td style="width: 158px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Prolonged toxicity due to saturation of elimination pathways</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>As the administered dose or plasma concentration increases, drug elimination may shift from first-order to mixed-order and eventually to zero-order kinetics as metabolic enzymes or transport pathways become progressively saturated.</p>
