<p>50 mg/L</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Rate constant</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>k0 = 5 mg/L/h</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>k1 = 0.15 (h<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Units of rate constant</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Amount/time (mg/L/h)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Reciprocal time (h<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elimination rate (at t=0)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant: 5 mg/L/h</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>dC/dt = 0.15 &#x000d7; 50 = 7.5 mg/L/h</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elimination rate behavior</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant</p>
<p>Independent of concentration</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Propotional to C</p>
<p>Changes with concentration</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Half-life (t&#x000bd;)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>At C0 = 50: t&#x000bd; = 5 h</p>
<p>Variable</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>0.693&#x000a0;/ 0.15 = 4.6 h</p>
<p>Constant</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Half-life</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Depends on initial concentration</p>
<p>Changes with dose</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Independent of dose or C</p>
<p>Constant 4.6 h</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clearance at Co</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CL = 5&#x000a0;/ 50 = 0.1 L/h</p>
<p>Variable</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>CL = 0.15 &#x000d7; Vd</p>
<p>CONSTANT</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clearance behavior</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>DECREASES as C increases</p>
<p>Inverse relationship: CL = k0/C</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Independent of C</p>
<p>Fixed property: CL = k1 &#x000d7; Vd</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Total elimination time</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>50&#x000a0;/ 5 = 10 h</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Theoretically infinite. At 5 t&#x000bd; (23 h): 97% eliminated</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Curve shape</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Linear (straight line)</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Exponential (curved line)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 175px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Dose-response relationship</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 178px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Nonlinear - disproportional</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 330.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Linear- proportional</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>The pharmacokinetics calculations above correspond to the&#x000a0;<bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;First- versus Zero-Order Kinetics. The red line indicates zero-order kinetics, whereas the blue line represents first-order kinetics. The calculations and concept are given in the table above for side-by-side comparison.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-31464.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4.&#x000a0;Rapid Overview of Zero- and First-Order Kinetics</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="border-color: black; float: left; width: 671px;" border="black">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Pharmacokinetics</bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold><bold>Zero-Order&#x000a0;Kinetics</bold></bold></p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>First-Order Kinetics</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Elimination rate</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant amount&#x000a0;eliminated per unit time, regardless of concentration</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant proportion&#x000a0;(or fraction) eliminated per unit time, proportional to concentration</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Half-life</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Variable (&#x02191; with C)</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clearance</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Variable (&#x02193; with C)</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Constant</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Dose-response</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Nonlinear</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Linear</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35px;">
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Therapeutic index</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Narrow (toxicity risk)</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Wider (more predictable)</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 35.5555px;">
                <td style="height: 35.5555px; width: 174px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Clinical examples</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.5555px; width: 233.917px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Phenytoin, alcohol, and salicylates (at high doses)</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 35.5555px; width: 239.083px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Most other drugs</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31464.s7" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Pathophysiological conditions can shift drug kinetics from first-order toward zero-order or mixed-order. Hepatic diseases such as cirrhosis, fibrosis, or cholestasis reduce CYP-mediated metabolism, leading to the accumulation of hepatically metabolized drugs such as phenytoin, theophylline, and acetaminophen. In these disease states, saturable enzymes can reach capacity at lower concentrations, producing zero-order kinetics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Renal pathology also alters drug clearance. Acute tubular necrosis, glomerulonephritis, or chronic kidney disease can reduce filtration, secretion, or reabsorption efficiency, prolonging the half-life of renally eliminated drugs such as aminoglycosides, digoxin, and lithium. Saturation of remaining transporters may induce nonlinear elimination, thereby compounding the risk of toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Genetic polymorphisms in CYP enzymes or renal transporters produce interindividual variability in clearance. For instance, CYP2C9 or CYP2C19 variants reduce the metabolism of certain drugs, predisposing patients to toxicity. When combined with organ impairment, these variations can shift elimination from predictable first-order kinetics to nonlinear or zero-order kinetics.</p>
        <p>Systemic factors, such as hypoperfusion, inflammation, and sepsis, can further&#x000a0;impair hepatic metabolism and renal elimination, causing commonly first-order drugs to behave unpredictably. For this reason, frequent plasma monitoring and individualized dosing are essential in critically ill patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r31">[31]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-31464.s8" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Understanding elimination kinetics is vital for patient care, dosing strategies, and toxicity prevention. Most drugs follow first-order kinetics. For example, beta-lactam antibiotics exhibit predictable exponential decay, allowing clinicians to anticipate plasma concentrations and adjust doses safely. However, drugs with zero-order kinetics, such as ethanol, salicylates, phenytoin, methanol, and theophylline, can accumulate rapidly even at moderate doses. Zero-order elimination also dictates&#x000a0;therapeutic options; in methanol poisoning, early intervention depends on the elapsed time rather than the total dose.</p>
        <p>High-dose chemotherapy protocols, such as ICE (ifosfamide, carboplatin, and etoposide), illustrate practical applications of elimination kinetics: early plasma monitoring identifies patients at risk of renal toxicity before conventional markers, such as serum creatinine, rise. Similarly, theophylline therapy requires careful monitoring to prevent underdosing or toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r32">[32]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Digoxin also has a narrow therapeutic index, especially in renal impairment, so therapeutic drug monitoring may be required to prevent toxicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Recognizing transitions between kinetic patterns enables individualized therapy, minimizes adverse effects, and optimizes outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-31464.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;Integrating knowledge of elimination kinetics into clinical practice improves medication safety, predicts toxic accumulation, adjusts for organ impairment, and supports effective management of high-risk drugs. These principles exemplify the intersection of physiology and pharmacology in modern medicine.</p>
      </sec>
